1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,316
Midnight, November nineteenth, and you
press Buy on GTA 6 for seventy-nine

2
00:00:04,316 --> 00:00:05,050
ninety-nine.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:06,687
The game starts downloading.

4
00:00:06,687 --> 00:00:07,760
The money doesn't.

5
00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:12,787
By the end of that day, fans had
spent three point seven nine billion

6
00:00:12,787 --> 00:00:14,243
dollars on this one game.

7
00:00:14,243 --> 00:00:18,329
Take-Two, the company that owns
Rockstar, calls it the biggest launch in

8
00:00:18,329 --> 00:00:19,607
entertainment history.

9
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:23,313
Here's the part most headlines skip.

10
00:00:23,313 --> 00:00:26,564
Out of your eighty dollars, the
publisher gets about fifty-six.

11
00:00:26,564 --> 00:00:28,291
And it doesn't get it that night.

12
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:31,260
Four hands touch your money before then.

13
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:35,960
This video follows each one and the
clock that decides when the money

14
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:36,820
actually lands.

15
00:00:37,933 --> 00:00:40,758
Picture your payment as
a bag of eighty coins:

16
00:00:40,758 --> 00:00:42,021
the take from a job.

17
00:00:42,021 --> 00:00:45,448
In GTA, a crew splits the
take at a table after a heist.

18
00:00:45,448 --> 00:00:48,155
Your eighty dollars
gets split the same way,

19
00:00:48,155 --> 00:00:49,713
just with more paperwork.

20
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:52,247
Four players sit at this table.

21
00:00:52,707 --> 00:00:54,607
The state, which adds tax on top.

22
00:00:55,187 --> 00:00:57,107
The console store that
sold you the download.

23
00:00:57,507 --> 00:00:59,147
The card system that moves the money.

24
00:00:59,587 --> 00:01:02,107
And the publisher, Take-Two, sitting last.

25
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:06,607
Most people guess the publisher takes
nearly all of it because the publisher

26
00:01:06,647 --> 00:01:07,267
made the game.

27
00:01:07,907 --> 00:01:10,647
It feels right since the
publisher paid for years of work.

28
00:01:11,527 --> 00:01:15,367
At this table, the publisher is
served last and gets what's left.

29
00:01:17,147 --> 00:01:18,768
One word to watch first.

30
00:01:18,768 --> 00:01:21,881
Three point seven nine
billion is retail sales:

31
00:01:21,881 --> 00:01:24,410
what buyers paid, counted at the till.

32
00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:28,626
Revenue is what a company keeps
after everyone else takes a cut.

33
00:01:28,626 --> 00:01:30,827
Headlines print the first number.

34
00:01:31,967 --> 00:01:33,450
First hand: the state.

35
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:37,059
In most US states, sales tax
rides on top of the price,

36
00:01:37,059 --> 00:01:41,247
so the bag is heavier than eighty
coins before anyone sits down.

37
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:46,256
In a state with seven percent tax,
that's about five dollars sixty.

38
00:01:46,256 --> 00:01:49,448
The store collects it, and
passes it to the government.

39
00:01:49,448 --> 00:01:51,842
It never belonged to anyone at the table.

40
00:01:51,842 --> 00:01:56,174
In a state with no sales tax, like
Oregon, the bag stays at exactly eighty.

41
00:01:58,067 --> 00:02:01,007
Second hand, and the big
one: the console store.

42
00:02:01,007 --> 00:02:03,287
It takes thirty percent of the price.

43
00:02:03,287 --> 00:02:06,707
On seventy-nine ninety-nine,
that's twenty-four dollars.

44
00:02:07,887 --> 00:02:09,647
That number isn't a secret.

45
00:02:09,647 --> 00:02:14,111
In the Epic versus Google trial,
Epic's chief executive confirmed that

46
00:02:14,111 --> 00:02:18,325
Nintendo, PlayStation and Xbox all
charge thirty percent on games.

47
00:02:19,487 --> 00:02:21,707
There's something you need
to know about console makers.

48
00:02:22,647 --> 00:02:25,547
They sell the console itself
cheaply, often at a loss,

49
00:02:26,007 --> 00:02:28,567
and earn it back later from
every game sold in their store.

50
00:02:29,467 --> 00:02:31,027
The console gets you through the door.

51
00:02:32,067 --> 00:02:34,207
The store is where the
house makes its money.

52
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:35,987
Scale it up.

53
00:02:35,987 --> 00:02:39,009
If ten million copies sold
through one store at full price,

54
00:02:39,009 --> 00:02:42,281
that store's cut would be two
hundred and forty million dollars,

55
00:02:42,281 --> 00:02:43,692
from a game it didn't make.

56
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:46,413
Which store matters too.

57
00:02:46,413 --> 00:02:50,996
Analysts at Alinea estimate PlayStation
took about three of every four

58
00:02:50,996 --> 00:02:52,998
copies sold on launch weekend.

59
00:02:52,998 --> 00:02:56,160
A boxed copy from a shop
takes a different road,

60
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,547
where the shop keeps its own margin.

61
00:02:58,547 --> 00:03:02,220
To keep the bag simple, this
video follows the download.

62
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:06,480
Third hand: the card, and a twist.

63
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:09,608
Who pays the card fee
on your eighty dollars?

64
00:03:09,608 --> 00:03:10,560
Not Rockstar.

65
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:14,420
When you buy from a console
store, the store is the seller.

66
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:18,120
Its name is on your receipt, and
it's the one the card system charges.

67
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:21,460
That role has a name,
the merchant of record.

68
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:26,572
So the card fee comes out of
the store's twenty-four dollars.

69
00:03:26,572 --> 00:03:30,096
On a typical US card, that
fee is a couple of percent,

70
00:03:30,096 --> 00:03:31,763
roughly two dollars here.

71
00:03:31,763 --> 00:03:36,057
A company Sony's size likely pays
less, but its rate isn't public.

72
00:03:37,167 --> 00:03:40,238
Those two dollars have their
own split, between your bank,

73
00:03:40,238 --> 00:03:42,842
the card network and the
store's payment company.

74
00:03:42,842 --> 00:03:45,183
That one gets its own
video on this channel.

75
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:50,996
Fourth hand: the publisher, which gets
what's left after the store's cut,

76
00:03:50,996 --> 00:03:53,246
fifty-five dollars and ninety-nine cents.

77
00:03:53,246 --> 00:03:54,532
That's seventy percent.

78
00:03:55,647 --> 00:03:59,047
It sounds like plenty until you
put it next to what the game cost.

79
00:04:00,007 --> 00:04:03,767
Reports put GTA 6's budget at
more than a billion dollars,

80
00:04:04,247 --> 00:04:05,587
built over more than a decade.

81
00:04:06,447 --> 00:04:08,227
Rockstar has never confirmed a number.

82
00:04:09,347 --> 00:04:13,702
At fifty-six dollars a copy, a billion
dollars takes about eighteen million

83
00:04:13,702 --> 00:04:15,306
downloads just to pay back.

84
00:04:15,306 --> 00:04:16,738
That's before marketing.

85
00:04:16,738 --> 00:04:20,863
Eighteen million is roughly everyone
in the Netherlands buying one copy

86
00:04:20,863 --> 00:04:21,207
each.

87
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:26,609
Take-Two says thirty-two and a half
million copies were sold before the game

88
00:04:26,609 --> 00:04:27,448
even launched.

89
00:04:27,448 --> 00:04:30,303
Even with boxed copies and
lower margins mixed in,

90
00:04:30,303 --> 00:04:32,200
that's far past eighteen million.

91
00:04:34,053 --> 00:04:37,373
So everyone at the table gets
paid, just not on launch night.

92
00:04:38,487 --> 00:04:43,506
Three point seven nine billion in a
single day works out to about forty-four

93
00:04:43,506 --> 00:04:45,526
thousand dollars every second.

94
00:04:45,526 --> 00:04:48,982
Five times what GTA 5 made
on its first day in 2013.

95
00:04:48,982 --> 00:04:53,936
Take that single day, and it roughly
matches what the last two Avatar films

96
00:04:53,936 --> 00:04:55,107
grossed combined.

97
00:04:56,233 --> 00:04:58,593
But as the last video
on this channel showed,

98
00:04:59,113 --> 00:05:00,213
approved is not paid.

99
00:05:01,133 --> 00:05:03,713
When your card says approved,
your bank has made a promise.

100
00:05:04,293 --> 00:05:08,393
The money itself moves later in
batches, usually in one to three days.

101
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:12,720
So for a purchase made at midnight,
your coins were still sitting at your

102
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:13,060
bank.

103
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:14,920
The store had a promise.

104
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,300
Take-Two had a number on a sales counter.

105
00:05:18,433 --> 00:05:21,321
Days later, the card money
settles with the store.

106
00:05:21,321 --> 00:05:25,342
Then the store pays the publisher,
on a payout schedule written into a

107
00:05:25,342 --> 00:05:27,608
contract the public never gets to read.

108
00:05:27,608 --> 00:05:31,912
If that schedule pays once a month, a
sale on November nineteenth might not

109
00:05:31,912 --> 00:05:33,951
reach the publisher until December.

110
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:37,642
That's how a company can
announce a record day,

111
00:05:37,642 --> 00:05:39,586
while its money is still travelling.

112
00:05:39,586 --> 00:05:40,637
The trophy is real.

113
00:05:40,637 --> 00:05:42,318
The coins are still on a truck.

114
00:05:44,147 --> 00:05:45,536
Is thirty percent a rip-off?

115
00:05:45,536 --> 00:05:47,307
Someone took that question to court.

116
00:05:48,447 --> 00:05:52,872
In London, a class action on behalf
of about twelve million PlayStation

117
00:05:52,872 --> 00:05:56,191
users argued that the store's
cut made prices unfair.

118
00:05:56,191 --> 00:05:59,694
On November seventeenth, two
days before GTA 6 launched,

119
00:05:59,694 --> 00:06:01,784
the tribunal threw the claim out.

120
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:05,200
And here's the store's side, told fairly.

121
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,720
It built the console, it runs the
servers that deliver a game this size

122
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:12,620
overnight, and it handles
refunds and fraud.

123
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:17,280
Epic's finance chief said in court
that the cut is needed because consoles

124
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:18,520
are sold at a loss.

125
00:06:19,667 --> 00:06:23,628
That's the same Epic that sued Apple
and Google over their thirty percent.

126
00:06:23,628 --> 00:06:27,007
On consoles, it called the market
competitive and paid the cut.

127
00:06:28,833 --> 00:06:29,984
Back to the table.

128
00:06:29,984 --> 00:06:34,044
From your seventy-nine ninety-nine,
twenty-four went to the store,

129
00:06:34,044 --> 00:06:36,892
and about two of that
went to the card system.

130
00:06:36,892 --> 00:06:38,892
Fifty-six went to the publisher.

131
00:06:38,892 --> 00:06:40,951
The tax on top went to the state.

132
00:06:42,813 --> 00:06:44,833
And none of it landed the
night you pressed buy.

133
00:06:45,413 --> 00:06:46,713
The split happens at the table.

134
00:06:47,193 --> 00:06:48,533
The payment happens on the clock.

135
00:06:50,367 --> 00:06:52,307
So the next time a game
makes billions in a day,

136
00:06:52,727 --> 00:06:55,927
ask two things: who keeps
it, and when does it land?

137
00:06:57,747 --> 00:06:59,903
And those two dollars
the card system took?

138
00:06:59,903 --> 00:07:01,618
Your bank keeps the biggest piece.

139
00:07:01,618 --> 00:07:02,745
That's the next video.
